{:db/id 17592186050240 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id 17592186073776 :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Solutions to a System" :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186073480 :question/math-academy-id "e-14009" :question/problem "Show that the following system of equations has no solution.\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x + 2y &=4 \\\\ 2x + y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+2y &=4 \\\\ 2x+y &=4 && \\text{multiply by } -2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 2y &= 4 \\\\\n- 4x - 2y &= -8 \\\\\n0 &= (4 - 8) \\\\\n0 &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=-4$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*.\n\nGeometrically, this means that the corresponding lines *do not intersect*. In other words, they are *parallel.*\n\n![](images/04/048c377f99093f493c37bc4de0363527996c8928348b43ad9c9747965f984c1f.png)"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186074059 :question/math-academy-id "q-211984" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x + 4y &=6 \\\\ - 2x - y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}."} {:db/id 17592186074086 :question/math-academy-id "q-237043" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y - 3x - 2 &=0 \\\\ 4y - 12x - 8 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}."} {:db/id 17592186074087 :question/math-academy-id "q-237046" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - 5y &=15 \\\\ 4x - 10y &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}."} {:db/id 17592186529261 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{-2x + y = 6; x - 2y = 6$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $2$, then we will get a $2x$ term that can cancel with the $-2x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\{-2x + y = 6; x - 2y = 6 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (2)\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x + y &= 6 \\\\\n2x - 4y &= 12 \\\\\n-3y &= (6 + 12) \\\\\n-3y &= 18 \\\\\ny &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x + (1)(-6) &= 6 \\\\\n-2x &= 12 \\\\\nx &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(-6,-6)$."} {:db/id 17592186529263 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{y + 2x + 4 = 0; 4y - 4x + 9 = 0$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nFirst, rearrange each equation to put the variable terms on the left and the constant on the right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + y &= -4 \\\\\n-4x + 4y &= -9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $4x$ term that can cancel with the $-4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{2x + y = -4 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (2); -4x + 4y = -9\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 2y &= -8 \\\\\n-4x + 4y &= -9 \\\\\n6y &= (-8 - 9) \\\\\n6y &= -17 \\\\\ny &= - \\frac{17}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + (1)(- \\frac{17}{6}) &= -4 \\\\\n2x &= - \\frac{7}{6} \\\\\nx &= - \\frac{7}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(- \\frac{7}{12},- \\frac{17}{6})$."} {:db/id 17592186529265 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{-4x + 4y = -9; 2x - y = -4$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $2$, then we will get a $4x$ term that can cancel with the $-4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\{-4x + 4y = -9; 2x - y = -4 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (2)\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + 4y &= -9 \\\\\n4x - 2y &= -8 \\\\\n2y &= (-9 - 8) \\\\\n2y &= -17 \\\\\ny &= - \\frac{17}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + (4)(- \\frac{17}{2}) &= -9 \\\\\n-4x &= 25 \\\\\nx &= - \\frac{25}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(- \\frac{25}{4},- \\frac{17}{2})$."} {:db/id 17592186529266 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{y + 2x + 2 = 0; 4y + 8x + 8 = 0$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nFirst, rearrange each equation to put the variable terms on the left and the constant on the right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + y &= -2 \\\\\n8x + 4y &= -8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-4$, then we will get a $-8x$ term that can cancel with the $8x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{2x + y = -2 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-4); 8x + 4y = -8\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-8x - 4y &= 8 \\\\\n8x + 4y &= -8 \\\\\n0 &= (8 - 8) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=0$ is *always true*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *infinitely many solutions*."} {:db/id 17592186529271 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{2x + y = 2; 4x + 2y = 5$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{2x + y = 2 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-2); 4x + 2y = 5\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x - 2y &= -4 \\\\\n4x + 2y &= 5 \\\\\n0 &= (-4 + 5) \\\\\n0 &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=1$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*."} {:db/id 17592186529284 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{y - 3x + 1 = 0; -3y - 6x - 8 = 0$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nFirst, rearrange each equation to put the variable terms on the left and the constant on the right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x + y &= -1 \\\\\n-6x - 3y &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $6x$ term that can cancel with the $-6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{-3x + y = -1 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-2); -6x - 3y = 8\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - 2y &= 2 \\\\\n-6x - 3y &= 8 \\\\\n-5y &= (2 + 8) \\\\\n-5y &= 10 \\\\\ny &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x + (1)(-2) &= -1 \\\\\n-3x &= 1 \\\\\nx &= - \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(- \\frac{1}{3},-2)$."} {:db/id 17592186529290 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{2x - 3y = 10; 2x - y = 6$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-1$, then we will get a $-2x$ term that can cancel with the $2x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{2x - 3y = 10 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-1); 2x - y = 6\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x + 3y &= -10 \\\\\n2x - y &= 6 \\\\\n2y &= (-10 + 6) \\\\\n2y &= -4 \\\\\ny &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + (-3)(-2) &= 10 \\\\\n2x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(2,-2)$."} {:db/id 17592186529293 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{2x - y = -2; 8x - 4y = -8$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-4$, then we will get a $-8x$ term that can cancel with the $8x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{2x - y = -2 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-4); 8x - 4y = -8\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-8x + 4y &= 8 \\\\\n8x - 4y &= -8 \\\\\n0 &= (8 - 8) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=0$ is *always true*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *infinitely many solutions*."} {:db/id 17592186529294 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{3x + y = 0; -3x + 5y = -2$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $1$, then we will get a $3x$ term that can cancel with the $-3x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{3x + y = 0 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (1); -3x + 5y = -2\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + y &= 0 \\\\\n-3x + 5y &= -2 \\\\\n6y &= (0 - 2) \\\\\n6y &= -2 \\\\\ny &= - \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + (1)(- \\frac{1}{3}) &= 0 \\\\\n3x &= \\frac{1}{3} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(\\frac{1}{9},- \\frac{1}{3})$."} {:db/id 17592186529306 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{-3x - y = 8; 3x + y = -6$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $1$, then we will get a $-3x$ term that can cancel with the $3x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{-3x - y = 8 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (1); 3x + y = -6\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x - y &= 8 \\\\\n3x + y &= -6 \\\\\n0 &= (8 - 6) \\\\\n0 &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=2$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*."} {:db/id 17592186529307 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{y - 3x - 4 = 0; -2y + 12x + 10 = 0$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nFirst, rearrange each equation to put the variable terms on the left and the constant on the right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x + y &= 4 \\\\\n12x - 2y &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $4$, then we will get a $-12x$ term that can cancel with the $12x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{-3x + y = 4 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (4); 12x - 2y = -10\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12x + 4y &= 16 \\\\\n12x - 2y &= -10 \\\\\n2y &= (16 - 10) \\\\\n2y &= 6 \\\\\ny &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x + (1)(3) &= 4 \\\\\n-3x &= 1 \\\\\nx &= - \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(- \\frac{1}{3},3)$."} {:db/id 17592186529309 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{y + 3x + 6 = 0; -4y - 12x - 26 = 0$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nFirst, rearrange each equation to put the variable terms on the left and the constant on the right:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + y &= -6 \\\\\n-12x - 4y &= 26\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $4$, then we will get a $12x$ term that can cancel with the $-12x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{3x + y = -6 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (4); -12x - 4y = 26\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x + 4y &= -24 \\\\\n-12x - 4y &= 26 \\\\\n0 &= (-24 + 26) \\\\\n0 &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=2$ is *always false*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *no solutions*."} {:db/id 17592186529316 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{-x - 5y = -12; x - 3y = -2$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $1$, then we will get a $-x$ term that can cancel with the $x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{-x - 5y = -12 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (1); x - 3y = -2\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x - 5y &= -12 \\\\\nx - 3y &= -2 \\\\\n-8y &= (-12 - 2) \\\\\n-8y &= -14 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{7}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x + (-5)(\\frac{7}{4}) &= -12 \\\\\n-x &= - \\frac{13}{4} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{13}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(\\frac{13}{4},\\frac{7}{4})$."} {:db/id 17592186529318 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{x - 3y = -6; 4x - y = -12$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-4$, then we will get a $-4x$ term that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{x - 3y = -6 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-4); 4x - y = -12\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, the $x$ terms cancel, and we can solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + 12y &= 24 \\\\\n4x - y &= -12 \\\\\n11y &= (24 - 12) \\\\\n11y &= 12 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{12}{11}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + (-3)(\\frac{12}{11}) &= -6 \\\\\nx &= - \\frac{30}{11} \\\\\nx &= - \\frac{30}{11}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system has *a single solution*, $(- \\frac{30}{11},\\frac{12}{11})$."} {:db/id 17592186529320 :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$\\{2x - y = 4; 2x - y = 4$\n\nThis system of equations has {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by solving the system using elimination.\n\nNotice that if we multiply the first equation by $-1$, then we will get a $-2x$ term that can cancel with the $2x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\{2x - y = 4 \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-1); 2x - y = 4\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x + y &= -4 \\\\\n2x - y &= 4 \\\\\n0 &= (-4 + 4) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe statement $0=0$ is *always true*. Therefore, we conclude that the system has *infinitely many solutions*."}]} {:db/id 17592186073777 :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Number of Intersection Points of Two Lines" :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186073481 :question/math-academy-id "e-14011" :question/problem "At how many points do the lines $x - 2y = 2$ and $10y - 5x = -10$ intersect?" :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 2y &=2 \\\\ 10y - 5x &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $x$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= 2 \\\\\nx &= 2y + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\nx=2y+2\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10y - 5x &= -10 \\\\\n10y - 5(2y + 2) &= -10 \\\\\n10y - 10y - 10 &= -10 \\\\\n- 10 &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)."} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186074060 :question/math-academy-id "q-211986" :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: 2x - y =-2, l_{2}: 6x - 3y =-12$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}."} {:db/id 17592186074089 :question/math-academy-id "q-237095" :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: y = 3x - 4, l_{2}: 12x - 4y = 16$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}."} {:db/id 17592186561203 :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: y = x + 6, l_{2}: -3x + 3y = 18$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &= x + 6 \\\\ -3x + 3y &= 18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. The first equation already gives $y$ in terms of $x$:\n\n$$\ny = x + 6\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=x + 6\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x + 3y &= 18 \\\\\n-3x + 3\\left(x + 6\\right) &= 18 \\\\\n-3x + 3x + 18 &= 18 \\\\\n18 &= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)."} {:db/id 17592186561213 :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: 5x - 2y =-1, l_{2}: -5x + 2y = 1$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x - 2y &=-1 \\\\ -5x + 2y &= 1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x - 2y &=-1 \\\\\n-2y &= -5x - 1 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-5x - 1}{-2} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x + 2y &= 1 \\\\\n-5x + 2\\left(\\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}\\right) &= 1 \\\\\n-5x + 5x + 1 &= 1 \\\\\n1 &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)."} {:db/id 17592186561216 :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: 2x - 3y = 0, l_{2}: 6x - 9y = 0$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - 3y &= 0 \\\\ 6x - 9y &= 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - 3y &= 0 \\\\\n-3y &= -2x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-2x}{-3} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2}{3}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}x\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - 9y &= 0 \\\\\n6x - 9\\left(\\frac{2}{3}x\\right) &= 0 \\\\\n6x - 6x &= 0 \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)."} {:db/id 17592186561218 :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: -5x + y =-1, l_{2}: 10x - 2y = 2$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x + y &=-1 \\\\ 10x - 2y &= 2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x + y &=-1 \\\\\ny &= 5x - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=5x - 1\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10x - 2y &= 2 \\\\\n10x - 2\\left(5x - 1\\right) &= 2 \\\\\n10x - 10x + 2 &= 2 \\\\\n2 &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)."} {:db/id 17592186561222 :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: y = -5x - 5, l_{2}: 25x + 5y =-20$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &= -5x - 5 \\\\ 25x + 5y &=-20\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. The first equation already gives $y$ in terms of $x$:\n\n$$\ny = -5x - 5\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=-5x - 5\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n25x + 5y &=-20 \\\\\n25x + 5\\left(-5x - 5\\right) &= -20 \\\\\n25x - 25x - 25 &= -20 \\\\\n-25 &= -20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always false. Therefore, we conclude that the system has no solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines do not intersect (i.e., they are parallel)."} {:db/id 17592186561223 :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: -2x - 3y =-4, l_{2}: 12x + 20y = 6$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x - 3y &=-4 \\\\ 12x + 20y &= 6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x - 3y &=-4 \\\\\n-3y &= 2x - 4 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2x - 4}{-3} \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{2}{3}x + \\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{2}{3}x + \\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x + 20y &= 6 \\\\\n12x + 20\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}x + \\frac{4}{3}\\right) &= 6 \\\\\n12x - \\frac{40}{3}x + \\frac{80}{3} &= 6 \\\\\n-\\frac{4}{3}x + \\frac{80}{3} &= 6 \\\\\n-\\frac{4}{3}x &= - \\frac{62}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ is nonzero, so this equation determines exactly one value of $x$. The first equation then determines exactly one value of $y$. Therefore, the system has one solution.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at one point."} {:db/id 17592186561226 :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: -4x + y = 2, l_{2}: -12x + 3y =-15$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x + y &= 2 \\\\ -12x + 3y &=-15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + y &= 2 \\\\\ny &= 4x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=4x + 2\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12x + 3y &=-15 \\\\\n-12x + 3\\left(4x + 2\\right) &= -15 \\\\\n-12x + 12x + 6 &= -15 \\\\\n6 &= -15\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always false. Therefore, we conclude that the system has no solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines do not intersect (i.e., they are parallel)."} {:db/id 17592186561236 :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: -x - y = 4, l_{2}: -12x - 3y =-12$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x - y &= 4 \\\\ -12x - 3y &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x - y &= 4 \\\\\n-y &= x + 4 \\\\\ny &= -x - 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=-x - 4\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12x - 3y &=-12 \\\\\n-12x - 3\\left(-x - 4\\right) &= -12 \\\\\n-12x + 3x + 12 &= -12 \\\\\n-9x + 12 &= -12 \\\\\n-9x &= -24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ is nonzero, so this equation determines exactly one value of $x$. The first equation then determines exactly one value of $y$. Therefore, the system has one solution.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at one point."} {:db/id 17592186561237 :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: y = -x - 4, l_{2}: -9x - 3y = 15$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &= -x - 4 \\\\ -9x - 3y &= 15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. The first equation already gives $y$ in terms of $x$:\n\n$$\ny = -x - 4\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=-x - 4\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-9x - 3y &= 15 \\\\\n-9x - 3\\left(-x - 4\\right) &= 15 \\\\\n-9x + 3x + 12 &= 15 \\\\\n-6x + 12 &= 15 \\\\\n-6x &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ is nonzero, so this equation determines exactly one value of $x$. The first equation then determines exactly one value of $y$. Therefore, the system has one solution.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at one point."} {:db/id 17592186561242 :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: -5x - y = 5, l_{2}: -20x - 4y =-8$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x - y &= 5 \\\\ -20x - 4y &=-8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x - y &= 5 \\\\\n-y &= 5x + 5 \\\\\ny &= -5x - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=-5x - 5\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-20x - 4y &=-8 \\\\\n-20x - 4\\left(-5x - 5\\right) &= -8 \\\\\n-20x + 20x + 20 &= -8 \\\\\n20 &= -8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always false. Therefore, we conclude that the system has no solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines do not intersect (i.e., they are parallel)."} {:db/id 17592186561247 :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: y = 4x + 2, l_{2}: -8x + 2y = 6$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &= 4x + 2 \\\\ -8x + 2y &= 6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. The first equation already gives $y$ in terms of $x$:\n\n$$\ny = 4x + 2\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=4x + 2\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-8x + 2y &= 6 \\\\\n-8x + 2\\left(4x + 2\\right) &= 6 \\\\\n-8x + 8x + 4 &= 6 \\\\\n4 &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always false. Therefore, we conclude that the system has no solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines do not intersect (i.e., they are parallel)."} {:db/id 17592186561248 :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: 5x + y =-1, l_{2}: -12x + 3y = 3$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x + y &=-1 \\\\ -12x + 3y &= 3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x + y &=-1 \\\\\ny &= -5x - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=-5x - 1\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12x + 3y &= 3 \\\\\n-12x + 3\\left(-5x - 1\\right) &= 3 \\\\\n-12x - 15x - 3 &= 3 \\\\\n-27x - 3 &= 3 \\\\\n-27x &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ is nonzero, so this equation determines exactly one value of $x$. The first equation then determines exactly one value of $y$. Therefore, the system has one solution.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at one point."} {:db/id 17592186561250 :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: y = x, l_{2}: -5x + 5y = 0$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &= x \\\\ -5x + 5y &= 0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. The first equation already gives $y$ in terms of $x$:\n\n$$\ny = x\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=x\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x + 5y &= 0 \\\\\n-5x + 5\\left(x\\right) &= 0 \\\\\n-5x + 5x &= 0 \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always true. Therefore, we conclude that the system has infinitely many solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines intersect at infinitely many points (i.e., they coincide)."} {:db/id 17592186561264 :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: -5x - 2y =-5, l_{2}: 20x + 8y = 21$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x - 2y &=-5 \\\\ 20x + 8y &= 21\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5x - 2y &=-5 \\\\\n-2y &= 5x - 5 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{5x - 5}{-2} \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20x + 8y &= 21 \\\\\n20x + 8\\left(-\\frac{5}{2}x + \\frac{5}{2}\\right) &= 21 \\\\\n20x - 20x + 20 &= 21 \\\\\n20 &= 21\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always false. Therefore, we conclude that the system has no solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines do not intersect (i.e., they are parallel)."} {:db/id 17592186561266 :question/problem "Consider the lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, defined as\n$l_{1}: 5x - 3y = 2, l_{2}: 20x - 12y = 7$.\n\nAt how many points do these lines intersect?\n\nThe lines $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The number of points of intersection between the two lines is equal to the number of solutions of the corresponding system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x - 3y &= 2 \\\\ 20x - 12y &= 7\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using substitution. First, we solve for $y$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x - 3y &= 2 \\\\\n-3y &= -5x + 2 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-5x + 2}{-3} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{5}{3}x - \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{3}x - \\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\ninto the second equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n20x - 12y &= 7 \\\\\n20x - 12\\left(\\frac{5}{3}x - \\frac{2}{3}\\right) &= 7 \\\\\n20x - 20x + 8 &= 7 \\\\\n8 &= 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result is a statement that's always false. Therefore, we conclude that the system has no solutions.\n\nConsequently, the lines do not intersect (i.e., they are parallel)."}]} {:db/id 17592186073778 :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a System With Infinitely Many Intersection Points" :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186073482 :question/math-academy-id "e-14014" :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $3x+2y=2$ and $6x+4y=4$." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x + 2y &=2 \\\\ 6x + 4y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+2y &=2 && \\text{multiply by } -2 \\\\ 6x+4y &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x - 4y &= -4 \\\\\n6x + 4y &= 4 \\\\\n0 &= (-4 + 4) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ is a solution, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x + 2y &= 2 \\\\\n2y &= 2 - 3x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2 - 3x}{2} \\\\\ny &= 1 - \\frac{3}{2}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,1-\\dfrac{3}{2}x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186074057 :question/math-academy-id "q-208832" :question/problem "Describe the points that lie on the intersection of the lines $x - 4y =-3$ and $2x - 8y =-6$. Express your answer in terms of $x$ only.The intersection points are $(x$,\n{{field-1}}\n$)$, where $x$ is any number."} {:db/id 17592186074138 :question/math-academy-id "q-25524" :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $9x - 3y =-6$ and $3x - y =-2$."} {:db/id 17592186542434 :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $4x + y = 8$ and $-8x - 2y =-16$." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x + y &=8 \\\\ -8x - 2y &=-16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $8x$ term that can cancel with the $-8x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x + y &=8 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (2) \\\\ -8x - 2y &=-16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n8x + 2y &= 16 \\\\\n-8x - 2y &= -16 \\\\\n0 &= (16 - 16) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + y &= 8 \\\\\ny &= 8 - 4x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{8 - 4x}{1} \\\\\ny &= 8 - 4x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(u,8 - 4u \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is any number."} {:db/id 17592186542435 :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $x - 4y = 3$ and $-4x + 16y =-12$." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 4y &=3 \\\\ -4x + 16y &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $4$, then we will get a $4x$ term that can cancel with the $-4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 4y &=3 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (4) \\\\ -4x + 16y &=-12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 16y &= 12 \\\\\n-4x + 16y &= -12 \\\\\n0 &= (12 - 12) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 4y &= 3 \\\\\n-4y &= 3 - x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{3 - x}{-4} \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{1}{4}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(u,-\\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{1}{4}u \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is any number."} {:db/id 17592186542442 :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $x - 2y = 10$ and $3x - 6y = 30$." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 2y &=10 \\\\ 3x - 6y &=30\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-3$, then we will get a $-3x$ term that can cancel with the $3x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x - 2y &=10 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-3) \\\\ 3x - 6y &=30\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x + 6y &= -30 \\\\\n3x - 6y &= 30 \\\\\n0 &= (-30 + 30) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx - 2y &= 10 \\\\\n-2y &= 10 - x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{10 - x}{-2} \\\\\ny &= -5 + \\frac{1}{2}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $t=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(t,-5 + \\frac{1}{2}t \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is any number."} {:db/id 17592186542448 :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $2x - y = 1$ and $-4x + 2y =-2$." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=1 \\\\ -4x + 2y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $4x$ term that can cancel with the $-4x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=1 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (2) \\\\ -4x + 2y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 2y &= 2 \\\\\n-4x + 2y &= -2 \\\\\n0 &= (2 - 2) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y &= 1 \\\\\n-y &= 1 - 2x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1 - 2x}{-1} \\\\\ny &= -1 + 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(u,-1 + 2u \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is any number."} {:db/id 17592186542452 :question/problem "Describe the points that lie on the intersection of the lines $2x - y =-6$ and $6x - 3y =-18$. Express your answer in terms of $t$ only.\n\nThe intersection points are $(t$,\n{{field-1}}\n$)$, where $t$ is any number." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=-6 \\\\ 6x - 3y &=-18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-3$, then we will get a $-6x$ term that can cancel with the $6x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x - y &=-6 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-3) \\\\ 6x - 3y &=-18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6x + 3y &= 18 \\\\\n6x - 3y &= -18 \\\\\n0 &= (18 - 18) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x - y &= -6 \\\\\n-y &= -6 - 2x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-6 - 2x}{-1} \\\\\ny &= 6 + 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $t=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(t,6 + 2t \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is any number."} {:db/id 17592186542462 :question/problem "Describe the points that lie on the intersection of the lines $4x - 2y =-11$ and $8x - 4y =-22$. Express your answer in terms of $x$ only.\n\nThe intersection points are $(x$,\n{{field-1}}\n$)$, where $x$ is any number." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x - 2y &=-11 \\\\ 8x - 4y &=-22\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-2$, then we will get a $-8x$ term that can cancel with the $8x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x - 2y &=-11 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-2) \\\\ 8x - 4y &=-22\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-8x + 4y &= 22 \\\\\n8x - 4y &= -22 \\\\\n0 &= (22 - 22) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x - 2y &= -11 \\\\\n-2y &= -11 - 4x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-11 - 4x}{-2} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{11}{2} + 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,\\frac{11}{2} + 2x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."} {:db/id 17592186542465 :question/problem "Describe the points that lie on the intersection of the lines $-12x + 5y =-6$ and $24x - 10y = 12$. Express your answer in terms of $x$ only.\n\nThe intersection points are $(x$,\n{{field-1}}\n$)$, where $x$ is any number." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12x + 5y &=-6 \\\\ 24x - 10y &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $-24x$ term that can cancel with the $24x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-12x + 5y &=-6 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (2) \\\\ 24x - 10y &=12\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-24x + 10y &= -12 \\\\\n24x - 10y &= 12 \\\\\n0 &= (-12 + 12) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12x + 5y &= -6 \\\\\n5y &= -6 + 12x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-6 + 12x}{5} \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{6}{5} + \\frac{12}{5}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,-\\frac{6}{5} + \\frac{12}{5}x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."} {:db/id 17592186542470 :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $-x - 4y =-4$ and $2x + 8y = 8$." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x - 4y &=-4 \\\\ 2x + 8y &=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $-2x$ term that can cancel with the $2x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-x - 4y &=-4 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (2) \\\\ 2x + 8y &=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2x - 8y &= -8 \\\\\n2x + 8y &= 8 \\\\\n0 &= (-8 + 8) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x - 4y &= -4 \\\\\n-4y &= -4 + x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-4 + x}{-4} \\\\\ny &= 1 - \\frac{1}{4}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(u,1 - \\frac{1}{4}u \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is any number."} {:db/id 17592186542476 :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $-7x + 2y = 6$ and $-21x + 6y = 18$." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-7x + 2y &=6 \\\\ -21x + 6y &=18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-3$, then we will get a $21x$ term that can cancel with the $-21x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-7x + 2y &=6 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-3) \\\\ -21x + 6y &=18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n21x - 6y &= -18 \\\\\n-21x + 6y &= 18 \\\\\n0 &= (-18 + 18) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x + 2y &= 6 \\\\\n2y &= 6 + 7x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{6 + 7x}{2} \\\\\ny &= 3 + \\frac{7}{2}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,3 + \\frac{7}{2}x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."} {:db/id 17592186542477 :question/problem "Describe the points that lie on the intersection of the lines $7x + 3y = 10$ and $21x + 9y = 30$. Express your answer in terms of $t$ only.\n\nThe intersection points are $(t$,\n{{field-1}}\n$)$, where $t$ is any number." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7x + 3y &=10 \\\\ 21x + 9y &=30\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $-3$, then we will get a $-21x$ term that can cancel with the $21x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7x + 3y &=10 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (-3) \\\\ 21x + 9y &=30\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-21x - 9y &= -30 \\\\\n21x + 9y &= 30 \\\\\n0 &= (-30 + 30) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x + 3y &= 10 \\\\\n3y &= 10 - 7x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{10 - 7x}{3} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{10}{3} - \\frac{7}{3}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $t=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(t,\\frac{10}{3} - \\frac{7}{3}t \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is any number."} {:db/id 17592186542491 :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $-8x + 5y =-9$ and $16x - 10y = 18$." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8x + 5y &=-9 \\\\ 16x - 10y &=18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $-16x$ term that can cancel with the $16x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-8x + 5y &=-9 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (2) \\\\ 16x - 10y &=18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-16x + 10y &= -18 \\\\\n16x - 10y &= 18 \\\\\n0 &= (-18 + 18) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-8x + 5y &= -9 \\\\\n5y &= -9 + 8x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-9 + 8x}{5} \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{9}{5} + \\frac{8}{5}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(u,-\\frac{9}{5} + \\frac{8}{5}u \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is any number."} {:db/id 17592186542505 :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $6x - y = 4$ and $-24x + 4y =-16$." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x - y &=4 \\\\ -24x + 4y &=-16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $4$, then we will get a $24x$ term that can cancel with the $-24x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x - y &=4 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (4) \\\\ -24x + 4y &=-16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n24x - 4y &= 16 \\\\\n-24x + 4y &= -16 \\\\\n0 &= (16 - 16) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6x - y &= 4 \\\\\n-y &= 4 - 6x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{4 - 6x}{-1} \\\\\ny &= -4 + 6x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(x,-4 + 6x \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number."} {:db/id 17592186542507 :question/problem "Describe the points that lie on the intersection of the lines $7x + 2y = 10$ and $21x + 6y = 30$. Express your answer in terms of $t$ only.\n\nThe intersection points are $(t$,\n{{field-1}}\n$)$, where $t$ is any number." :quest