uation by $4$, then we will get a $-12x$ term that can cancel with the $12x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x - y &=-4 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (4) \\\\ 12x + 4y &=16\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-12x - 4y &= -16 \\\\\n12x + 4y &= 16 \\\\\n0 &= (-16 + 16) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x - y &= -4 \\\\\n-y &= -4 + 3x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{-4 + 3x}{-1} \\\\\ny &= 4 - 3x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $t=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(t,4 - 3t \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is any number."} {:db/id 17592186542510 :question/problem "Find all points of intersection of the lines $7x + 5y = 4$ and $-14x - 10y =-8$." :question/worked-solution "The points of intersection of the lines correspond to the solutions of the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7x + 5y &=4 \\\\ -14x - 10y &=-8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system using elimination. Notice that if we multiply the first equation by $2$, then we will get a $14x$ term that can cancel with the $-14x$ term in the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7x + 5y &=4 && \\mid \\mid \\cdot (2) \\\\ -14x - 10y &=-8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhen we add the two equations, it turns out that both variables cancel, and we obtain a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n14x + 10y &= 8 \\\\\n-14x - 10y &= -8 \\\\\n0 &= (8 - 8) \\\\\n0 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause we obtained a true statement, we conclude that there are infinitely many solutions to the system. Therefore, the lines have infinitely many intersection points.\n\nWe want to find an expression that represents every solution to the system. Any value of $x$ can be chosen, and we can find the corresponding $y$-coordinate for each $x$-coordinate by solving one of the lines for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7x + 5y &= 4 \\\\\n5y &= 4 - 7x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{4 - 7x}{5} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{4}{5} - \\frac{7}{5}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=x$ be the free coordinate.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are\n\n$$\n\\left(u,\\frac{4}{5} - \\frac{7}{5}u \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is any number."}]}]}
